# H15f — an out-of-range number is not "not a number"

Worktree `D:\Projects\Camel-wt\h15f-range`, branch `handover/H15f-range-text` (from `develop`).
Everything is left in the working tree: no commit, no new branch, no push.

This is the small item of the H15e review (`docs/handover/reviews/H10-H16.md`, line 35), answer 2:

> 2. **超出范围的数字**：消息说明超出范围（如"超出 `float` 的范围"），与"不是数字"区分；整数路径同样。小项。

Its design frame is design principles **5.1** (a literal takes its type from context; a value is
never wrapped or cut to fit a type) and **10.6** (a value that cannot be read is a located
error that says what is wrong), with `SemanticDiag::LiteralNotRepresentable`'s message as the
model: it quotes the text and gives the range.

---

## 1. The two causes, and where each is decided

Reading the text of a number has two failures, and they are now two codes and two sentences:

| cause | code | message |
|---|---|---|
| it is not a number at all (wrong form, trailing junk, blanks, empty text) | `RuntimeDiag::InvalidNumberText` (existing) | `Cannot convert the string '{0}' to {1}.` |
| it is a number, and the type cannot hold it (overflow **or** underflow) | `RuntimeDiag::ConversionOutOfRange` (existing) | `Cannot convert {0} to {1}: {2}.`, `{0}` the text quoted as a written value, `{2}` the range |

No code was invented: both codes existed and already meant exactly these two things (text that
is not a number; a conversion whose value does not fit). What changed is which one the text
paths use, and that the reader no longer assembles a phrase of its own
(`"… (the number is outside its range)"` is gone).

The decision is made once per reader, by the status the number parser returns
(`NumberTextStatus::{Ok, NotANumber, OutOfRange}`, `include/camel/utils/number_text.h`):

| path | decision | code |
|---|---|---|
| conversion calls `int(s)` / `i64` / `i32` / `float` / `f64` / `f32` (interpreter) | `camel::core::integerFromText<T>` / `floatFromText<T>`, `include/camel/core/numeric_convert.h` | the primitive |
| the same calls in `std::cpp` | `rt::integerFromText<T>` / `floatFromText<T>` (`include/camel/standalone/num.h`), with **two** sites (invalid, out of range) chosen by the same status; the operator rule is `checkedTextConversion` in `src/passes/trans/cpp/operator_rules.cpp` | the matching Site |
| `--arg` of an `int`/`float` parameter (interpreter) | `loadValue`, `src/execute/entry_args.cpp` | `InvalidNumberText` / `ConversionOutOfRange`, rendered through the new `runtimeDiagMessage` |
| `--arg` of a `c64`/`c128` parameter, each part (interpreter) | `complexFromText<T>`, same file: the reason names the part | the same two, with `the real|imaginary part is outside the range of …` |
| `--arg` of an `int`/`float`/`c64`/`c128` parameter (`std::cpp`) | `EntryArguments`, `include/camel/standalone/entry.h`; the sentences come pre-rendered from the catalogue in `EntryNumberText`, filled in by `program_emitter.cpp` | the same two |
| a conversion call whose argument is a text literal (`int('…')`) | `Builder::rejectLiteralConversion`, `src/compile/gir/builder_diagnostics.cpp` | `SemanticDiag::LiteralNotRepresentable`, reason `out of range (<range>)` or `the text is not a number` |

Two supporting fixes came with it:

- `parseWholeFloat`'s non-`from_chars` fallback treated only `inf` as out of range; a strtod
  underflow (result rounded to zero with `ERANGE`) now returns `OutOfRange` there too, so the
  two implementations agree. On this platform `from_chars` is used (MSVC 19); the fallback is
  for libc++ before 20.
- The range texts live once: `integerRangeText<T>()`, `floatRangeText<T>()`,
  `complexRangeText<T>()` in `number_text.h`; `literal::rangeText(Type*)` now delegates to
  them, and `numeric_convert.h`'s `integerRangeReason<T>`/`floatRangeReason<T>` build the reason
  `the value is outside the range of X (<range>)` from them.

## 2. The messages as they now read

Interpreter conversion, out of range (quoted text, target, range):

```text
error ConversionOutOfRange: Cannot convert '1e999' to float: the value is outside the range of float (at most about 1.8e308 in magnitude).
error ConversionOutOfRange: Cannot convert '99999999999999999999' to int: the value is outside the range of int (-9223372036854775808 to 9223372036854775807).
```

Not a number (unchanged sentence):

```text
error InvalidNumberText: Cannot convert the string '12abc' to int.
```

Entry argument, cause nested in the entry report (`EntryArgumentInvalid` ends the sentence; the
catalogued sentence's final period is dropped):

```text
Cannot bind the argument 'rate' of 'main': Cannot convert '1e9999' to float: the value is outside the range of float (at most about 1.8e308 in magnitude).
Cannot bind the argument 'z' of 'main': Cannot convert '1e9999+2i' to c128: the real part is outside the range of c128 (each part at most about 1.8e308 in magnitude).
Cannot bind the argument 'z' of 'main': Cannot convert the string 'abc' to a complex number (write 1+2i, -2i, 0.5-0.25i or a real number such as 1.5).
```

Text literal converted at compile time (unchanged diagnostic, new reason):

```text
The literal ''99999999999999999999'' cannot be represented as type 'int': out of range (-9223372036854775808 to 9223372036854775807).
The literal ''1e999'' cannot be represented as type 'float': out of range (at most about 1.8e308 in magnitude).
```

The catalogue keeps one message per cause: `ConversionOutOfRange`'s help gained a clause for
text (`out of range, which is not the same as text that is not a number: that is
InvalidNumberText`); `InvalidNumberText` is untouched. `std::cpp` renders the same catalogue
sentences at translation time (`runtimeDiagMessage` in `src/core/error/runtime.cpp`), so the
generated program says exactly what the interpreter says, byte for byte.

